questions.starter_code:
const once = (fn) => {
  // your code
};

let calls = 0;
const init = once(() => { calls++; return "done"; });
console.log(init(), init(), calls);

questions.answer:
const once = (fn) => {
  let called = false;
  let result;
  return (...args) => {
    if (!called) {
      called = true;
      result = fn(...args);
    }
    return result;
  };
};

let calls = 0;
const init = once(() => { calls++; return "done"; });
console.log(init(), init(), calls);
// done done 1

The flag and the cached value live in the closure created by once, so every call of the returned function sees the same state — that is the whole mechanism. Rest parameters forward whatever arguments the caller passes. Set called = true before invoking fn, so a throwing fn is still not retried (choose the opposite if you want retries on failure). A Map keyed by arguments would extend this into memoization.

questions.starter_code:
const compose = (...fns) => {
  // your code
};

const inc = (n) => n + 1;
const double = (n) => n * 2;
console.log(compose(inc, double)(3));

questions.answer:
const compose = (...fns) => (x) => fns.reduceRight((value, fn) => fn(value), x);

const inc = (n) => n + 1;
const double = (n) => n * 2;
console.log(compose(inc, double)(3)); // 7

compose reads right to left, like the maths notation f(g(x)): double(3) is 6, then inc(6) is 7. reduceRight starts from the rightmost function, which is why the result is 7 and not 8 (pipe, the left-to-right version, uses reduce and would give 8). The inner functions run in order, and each receives the previous return value. Composition is the backbone of point-free pipelines in functional JavaScript.

questions.question:
This code logs 'Kakade' immediately instead of after one second, and the value is wrong. Why?

function greet(name) { console.log("Hello", name); }
setTimeout(greet("Kakade"), 1000);

[1, 2, 3].map(console.log);

questions.answer:
const flatten = (arr) =>
  arr.reduce(
    (acc, value) => acc.concat(Array.isArray(value) ? flatten(value) : value),
    []
  );

console.log(flatten([1, [2, [3, [4]]]]));
// [ 1, 2, 3, 4 ]

The base case is implicit: when a value is not an array, concat appends it. The recursive case handles nested arrays, and reduce builds the result level by level. Starting with [] means the empty array input returns [], and concat keeps everything non-mutating. A loop with an explicit stack avoids recursion limits on very deep structures — this version throws RangeError if nesting exceeds the call stack.
